<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>, <a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 are two acts of the UK Parliament that limit the power of the House of Lords. The 1911 Act, passed after the Lords rejected the People's Budget, removed their veto over money bills and replaced their veto over other bills with a delay of two years, which the 1949 Act cut to one year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The power of the unelected House of Lords was limited by the <b>Parliament Acts</b> of 1911 and 1949, and most hereditary peers were removed in 1999.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Parliament Act</b> of 1911 ended the House of Lords’ absolute veto.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
